# Generates the sample document collection in data/ (run once; safe to delete afterwards).
import os
DOCS = [
("Information Retrieval Basics", "Information retrieval is the process of finding documents that satisfy a user's information need from a large collection. An IR system represents, stores, organizes and provides access to information items such as text, images, audio and video. Unlike a database query, retrieval results are approximate and ranked by relevance."),
("Inverted Index", "An inverted index maps each term to the list of documents that contain it, called a postings list. Instead of scanning every document, the search engine looks up the query terms in the index, which makes retrieval fast even for millions of documents. Postings often store the term frequency and the positions of each term."),
("Text Preprocessing", "Before indexing, text is processed in several steps. Tokenization splits text into words, stop-word removal drops common words like the and of, and stemming reduces words to their roots. The same processing must be applied to documents and queries so that their terms can match."),
("Tokenization", "Tokenization breaks a stream of text into tokens such as words and numbers. Decisions about punctuation, hyphens, case and apostrophes change what the index stores. Good tokenizers lowercase the text, and languages without spaces between words need special handling."),
("Stop Words and Stemming", "Stop words are very frequent words such as the, is and of that carry little meaning for ranking. Stemming strips suffixes so that running and runs both map to run. Stemming increases recall because more word forms match, but it can merge words that are unrelated."),
("TF-IDF Weighting", "TF-IDF scores a term in a document by combining term frequency with inverse document frequency. A term that appears often in a document is important to it, while a term that appears in every document is a poor discriminator. Documents are ranked by the combined weight of the query terms they contain."),
("BM25 Ranking Function", "BM25 is a probabilistic ranking function used by Lucene and Elasticsearch. It improves on plain TF-IDF by saturating term frequency and normalizing for document length. The parameters k1 and b control how quickly repeated terms stop adding to the score."),
("Vector Space Model", "The vector space model represents documents and queries as vectors of term weights. Relevance is estimated with the cosine similarity between the query vector and each document vector. The more meaningful terms a query and a document share, the higher their similarity and the higher the document rank."),
("Boolean Retrieval Model", "The boolean model matches documents using exact AND, OR and NOT operators. A document either matches or it does not, so there is no ranking of the results. It is precise for expert users such as librarians but frustrating for casual searchers."),
("Precision and Recall", "Precision is the fraction of retrieved documents that are relevant, and recall is the fraction of relevant documents that were retrieved. A search engine usually trades one against the other. Metrics such as mean average precision and NDCG evaluate the quality of ranked results."),
("Web Crawling", "A web crawler follows hyperlinks to discover and download pages for a search engine. Crawlers respect robots.txt, avoid duplicate pages and revisit pages to keep the index fresh. The downloaded pages are then processed and added to the index."),
("PageRank and Link Analysis", "PageRank ranks web pages using the link graph of the web. A page is important if many important pages link to it. Link analysis helped early web search engines improve ranking beyond simple keyword matching."),
("Query Expansion", "Query expansion adds related terms such as synonyms to the original query to improve recall. For example, a search for car can also look for automobile. Relevance feedback and thesauri are common sources of expansion terms."),
("Semantic Search", "Semantic search tries to understand the meaning of a query instead of matching keywords only. Neural embeddings place similar meanings close together in vector space, so a query about cheap flights can match a document about low-cost airlines."),
("Learning to Rank", "Learning to rank uses machine learning to order search results. Features such as the BM25 score, click data and page quality are combined by a trained model. This replaces hand-tuned ranking formulas with a model learned from relevance judgments."),
("Generative AI and Retrieval-Augmented Generation", "Large language models such as ChatGPT generate answers instead of listing links. Retrieval-augmented generation first retrieves relevant documents from a search index and then gives them to the model as context. This reduces hallucination and keeps answers grounded in sources."),
("Vector Databases and FAISS", "A vector database stores embeddings and finds nearest neighbors quickly. FAISS is a library for efficient similarity search over dense vectors. These tools power semantic search and recommendation by comparing meaning rather than exact words."),
("Lucene and Elasticsearch", "Apache Lucene is a Java library that builds inverted indexes and ranks documents with BM25. Elasticsearch is a distributed search engine built on Lucene that exposes a REST API. Developers use them to add full-text search to their applications."),
("Recommendation Systems", "Recommendation systems such as those of Netflix and Spotify suggest items without an explicit query. Collaborative filtering uses the behavior of similar users, while content-based methods use item features. Recommendation is closely related to information filtering."),
("Machine Learning Introduction", "Machine learning is a field of artificial intelligence where algorithms learn patterns from data instead of following hand-written rules. Supervised learning uses labeled examples, while unsupervised learning finds structure in unlabeled data. Machine learning powers modern ranking and recommendation."),
("Natural Language Processing", "Natural language processing lets computers analyze human language. Typical tasks include part-of-speech tagging, named entity recognition, translation and question answering. NLP techniques improve search by helping systems understand queries and documents."),
("Library Card Catalogs", "Before computers, libraries used card catalogs to organize books by author, title and subject. A librarian never reads every book; she relies on the index to find the right shelf. Early library automation was the first generation of information retrieval systems."),
("Data Retrieval versus Information Retrieval", "Data retrieval, as in a SQL database, returns exact matches for well-structured queries, and one wrong value can mean total failure. Information retrieval deals with loosely structured text and returns ranked, approximate results, where a small error rarely breaks the system."),
("Browsing versus Searching", "Searching has a focused goal and a user who knows what the repository holds. Browsing has a vague goal, for example scrolling through travel photos without a clear target. Interfaces such as search engines and photo galleries often support both behaviors."),
("Search Engine Architecture", "A search engine has an offline part and an online part. Offline, documents are crawled, processed and indexed. Online, the query is processed, the index is looked up and the candidate documents are ranked before the results are shown to the user."),
]
os.makedirs("data", exist_ok=True)
for i, (title, body) in enumerate(DOCS, 1):
    slug = "".join(c if c.isalnum() else "_" for c in title.lower()).strip("_")
    with open(f"data/{i:02d}_{slug}.txt", "w", encoding="utf-8") as f:
        f.write(f"Title: {title}\n\n{body}\n")
print(len(DOCS), "documents written")
